## Updates from February 13, 2026 — Devlog Day 10

Today we integrated **ChatGPT as the third AI provider** for the AI Coach. But it wasn't as simple as calling the OpenAI API — we discovered that Codex subscribers use a completely different endpoint, with a different API format (Responses API), mandatory SSE streaming, and a subtle parser bug that took hours to debug.

---

### 🎯 Why a Third Provider?

We already had Claude (priority 1) and GitHub Copilot (fallback). But we wanted users with a ChatGPT subscription to also be able to use their own account. The waterfall is now:

```
Claude → Copilot → ChatGPT → System credentials
```

Three different paths, one AI Coach.

---

### 🔐 OAuth PKCE with OpenAI

ChatGPT uses **OAuth 2.0 PKCE** — the same pattern as Claude, but with its own URLs:

```typescript
// Authorization URL
https://auth.openai.com/authorize?
  client_id=pdlLIX2Y72MIl2rhLhTE9VV9bN6LJ...
  &code_challenge=<SHA256_CHALLENGE>
  &code_challenge_method=S256
  &redirect_uri=https://tenk.oventlabs.com/api/chatgpt-oauth/callback
  &response_type=code
  &scope=openai.chat
  &state=<STATE>

// Token exchange
POST https://auth.openai.com/oauth/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
```

Unlike Claude (which uses JSON body for token exchange — something unique in the industry), OpenAI follows the OAuth standard with `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`.

The flow is clean: user authorizes at auth.openai.com → callback with code → exchange for access_token + refresh_token → save to DB.

---

### 🔍 The Discovery: Codex API ≠ OpenAI API

Here's where it gets interesting. Initially we tried calling the standard OpenAI endpoint:

```typescript
// ❌ DOESN'T WORK for Codex subscribers
POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions
→ Error: model not available for this subscription
```

It turns out that users with a **ChatGPT Plus/Codex** subscription don't have access to the traditional API. Instead, OpenAI exposes a different internal endpoint:

```typescript
// ✅ Correct endpoint for Codex
POST https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex/responses
```

The difference? This endpoint uses the **Responses API** (not the Chat Completions API), with a completely different format.

---

### 📡 Responses API vs. Chat Completions API

The Chat Completions API (the classic one) uses `messages[]`:

```json
{
  "model": "gpt-4o",
  "messages": [
    { "role": "system", "content": "You are a coach..." },
    { "role": "user", "content": "Hello" }
  ]
}
```

The Responses API uses `instructions` + `input[]`:

```json
{
  "model": "gpt-5.3-codex",
  "instructions": "You are a deliberate practice coach...",
  "input": [
    { "role": "user", "content": "Hello" },
    { "role": "assistant", "content": "Hi! How can I help?" },
    { "role": "user", "content": "How's my guitar going?" }
  ],
  "tools": [...],
  "stream": true
}
```

The system prompt moves to `instructions` (outside the message array), and history goes in `input[]`. We had to implement `convertToCodexInput()` to transform our internal format to the Responses format:

```typescript
function convertToCodexInput(messages: Message[]) {
  let instructions = '';
  const input: CodexInput[] = [];

  for (const msg of messages) {
    if (msg.role === 'system') {
      instructions += msg.content + '\n';
    } else if (msg.role === 'tool') {
      input.push({
        type: 'function_call_output',
        call_id: msg.tool_call_id,
        output: JSON.stringify(msg.content),
      });
    } else {
      input.push({ role: msg.role, content: msg.content });
    }
  }

  return { instructions, input };
}
```

Note how `tool_result` messages become `function_call_output` with a `call_id` instead of `tool_use_id`. Each API has its own way of representing the same thing.

---

### 📺 Mandatory SSE Streaming

Unlike Claude and Copilot (where we can choose streaming or not), the Codex endpoint **requires `stream: true`** — without streaming, it returns an empty body.

The response arrives as **Server-Sent Events (SSE)**:

```
data: {"type": "response.created", ...}
data: {"type": "response.in_progress", ...}
data: {"type": "response.output_item.added", "item": {"id": "fc_abc", "call_id": "call_xyz", "name": "get_journey_stats"}}
data: {"type": "response.function_call_arguments.delta", "item_id": "fc_abc", "delta": "{\"us"}
data: {"type": "response.function_call_arguments.delta", "item_id": "fc_abc", "delta": "erId\""}
data: {"type": "response.function_call_arguments.done", "item_id": "fc_abc", "arguments": "{\"userId\": \"user123\"}"}
data: {"type": "response.completed", "response": {"usage": {"input_tokens": 1234}}}
```

We implemented `parseCodexStream()` which accumulates deltas for each tool call and extracts text content.

---

### 🐛 The item_id vs. call_id Bug

This was the hardest bug to find. The first message always worked, but follow-up messages returned *"I couldn't generate a response."*

#### The Symptom
Logs showed:
```
content: null, toolCallsCount: 0, textPartsCount: 0
```

The model wanted to call tools (like `get_journey_stats`), but the parser captured nothing.

#### The Cause
Codex SSE events use **different fields** depending on the event type:

- `response.output_item.added` → contains `item.id` (e.g., `"fc_abc"`) and `item.call_id` (e.g., `"call_xyz"`)
- `response.function_call_arguments.delta` → contains `item_id` (e.g., `"fc_abc"`) — **NOT `call_id`**

Our original parser looked for `evt.call_id` in deltas, but that field **doesn't exist** in delta events. Only `item_id` exists. As a result, tool call arguments accumulated under an `undefined` key, and when assembling the final tool calls, nothing was found.

#### The Fix
We created an `itemIdToCallId` map that bridges both identifiers:

```typescript
const itemIdToCallId = new Map<string, string>();

// When output_item.added arrives, register the mapping
case 'response.output_item.added':
  if (item.type === 'function_call') {
    itemIdToCallId.set(item.id, item.call_id);
  }
  break;

// In deltas, use item_id as key
case 'response.function_call_arguments.delta':
  const key = evt.item_id || evt.call_id;
  argBuffers[key] = (argBuffers[key] || '') + evt.delta;
  break;

// When assembling the final tool call, resolve the real call_id
case 'response.function_call_arguments.done':
  const resolvedCallId = itemIdToCallId.get(evt.item_id) || evt.call_id;
  toolCalls.push({
    id: resolvedCallId,  // The call_id needed by the agent loop
    name: toolName,
    arguments: parsedArgs,
  });
  break;
```

The `call_id` is crucial because the agent loop needs it to send the `tool_result` back to the model in the next iteration.

---

### 🔑 Secret Header: chatgpt-account-id

The Codex endpoint requires a special header: `chatgpt-account-id`. This ID is not sent during authorization — you have to **extract it from the JWT** access token:

```typescript
function extractAccountIdFromJwt(token: string): string {
  const payload = JSON.parse(
    Buffer.from(token.split('.')[1], 'base64url').toString()
  );
  return payload['https://api.openai.com/auth']?.user_id
    || payload.sub;
}
```

Without this header, the API returns 403. Another undocumented detail.

---

### 📊 Comparison: Three Providers, Three Worlds

| Aspect | Claude | Copilot | ChatGPT/Codex |
|--------|--------|---------|---------------|
| Auth | OAuth PKCE | Device Flow | OAuth PKCE |
| Token exchange | JSON body (unique) | form-urlencoded | form-urlencoded |
| API endpoint | api.claude.ai | api.github.com → JWT | chatgpt.com/backend-api |
| API format | Messages API | Chat Completions | Responses API |
| Streaming | Optional | Optional | **Mandatory** |
| System prompt | `system` message | `system` message | `instructions` field |
| Tool results | `tool_result` + `tool_use_id` | `tool` + `tool_call_id` | `function_call_output` + `call_id` |
| Model | claude-sonnet-4.5 | claude-sonnet-4.5 | gpt-5.3-codex |
| Quirks | Undocumented `code#state` | 2-token system | `item_id` ≠ `call_id` |

Each provider has its own authentication universe, API format, and quirks. Our backend abstracts all of this — the agent runner doesn't know or care which provider responded.

---

### 🗑️ Delete Conversations

We also implemented **conversation deletion** for the AI Coach. Users can swipe-to-delete any conversation in the side list:

- Swipe right to left → red background with trash icon
- Confirmation dialog: *"Delete 'conversation title'?"*
- Daily Tips conversations CANNOT be deleted
- If the deleted conversation is the current one, the view is cleared

---

### 🔄 Fix: Real-Time Status Chip

We had a bug where the header status chip ("AI Connected/Disconnected") didn't update after connecting a provider from the chat screen. The user would connect ChatGPT → chat worked → but the chip still showed "Disconnected".

The cause: `EmbeddedAiChatPage` updated its internal state but didn't notify the parent `HomeWebLayout`. The fix was adding an `onConnectionChanged` callback that triggers `_checkAiStatus()` in the parent layout.

---

### 💡 What We Learned

1. **Not all OpenAI APIs are the same** — `api.openai.com` is for paid API keys, ChatGPT subscribers use an internal endpoint with a different format.
2. **Responses API ≠ Chat Completions API** — the system prompt goes in `instructions`, tool results are `function_call_output`, and streaming is mandatory.
3. **SSE IDs can be confusing** — `item_id` and `call_id` are different things pointing to the same tool call. Without mapping them, tool calls are silently lost.
4. **Production debug logging saves lives** — without detailed logs of each SSE event, finding the `item_id` bug would have been impossible.
5. **Three providers, one Coach** — the backend abstraction is what allows the agent to work the same regardless of who responds.

---

**Status:** TenK now has **three AI providers**: Claude, GitHub Copilot, and ChatGPT. The 4-level waterfall ensures the AI Coach always has a path. Each provider has its own auth, its own API format, and its own quirks — but from the user's perspective, it's all one coach that never fails. 🚀
